+### Time-boxed measurement
+
+A trace far longer than any run can replay has no natural end, and engines that
+differ by orders of magnitude in speed would cover different amounts of it in
+any fixed budget. `--warmup-secs W --duration-secs D` (YAML `replay.window`)
+therefore measures a fixed span of wall clock instead of a fixed amount of work:
+
+```text
+t0     = measured replay starts, after the prefix-cache preflight
+window = [t0 + W, t0 + W + D)       W >= 0, D > 0, both or neither
+```
+
+At `t0 + W + D` the run stops releasing rounds and sessions, abandons every
+in-flight request by dropping its HTTP stream (closing the connection is how an
+OpenAI-compatible server learns to abort it), stops waiting on tool sleeps,
+writes the summary, and exits 0. Those abandoned requests are logged with status
+`CANCELLED_AT_WINDOW_END` and are never failures; a real failure (HTTP error,
+malformed stream, integrity failure) is still `FAILED` and still counts.
+
+It is accepted only for text replay (sessions or independent requests) under
+`--arrival-mode saturated`, in one process. Under `trace-timed` the offered load
+is the trace's own timeline, so a window would measure whichever slice of the
+schedule it happened to cover; multimodal surfaces report whole outputs rather
+than token events; a dry run measures nothing; and process shards would each
+open their own window after their own preflight.
+
+Every measurement is on one monotonic clock, in milliseconds since t0:
+
+| Field | Windowed definition |
+|---|---|
+| `output_token_throughput_per_s` | Output tokens whose stream event arrived inside the window, divided by D. Each event's tokens all take that event's arrival time, so a multi-token (speculative) chunk counts in full at its own instant. Tokens of requests that later failed are excluded, as in the run-wide metric. Completed requests are *not* what is counted, which would favour short requests |
+| `ttft_ms_*` | Over requests whose HTTP send was inside the window. A request with a first output inside the window contributes its own TTFT (the same token-id-preferred value as its record). One still waiting at window end is **censored**: `window end - send` enters the percentiles as a lower bound and is counted in `window.ttft_censored_requests`, so a server cannot look fast by starving long prompts. A request that failed before any output has no TTFT, as without a window |
+| `tpot_ms_*` | Per request that did not fail, from its token-id events inside the window only: `(last - first in-window event) / tokens in the in-window events after the first`. At least two in-window events are needed. This is `token_delivery_tpot_ms` restricted to the window, so a stream wholly inside it gets exactly its own TPOT |
+| `ttft_token_id_steps` / `ttft_text_fallback_steps` | Uncensored windowed TTFTs by clock |
+| `run_duration_ms` | `D * 1000` |
+| `request_throughput_per_s` | `success_steps / D` |
+| `output_byte_throughput_per_s`, `audio_throughput_s_per_s` | `null`: both count completed responses |
+| `attempted_steps`, `success_steps`, `failed_steps` | Steps that reached a verdict *inside* the window, whenever sent. `attempted = success + failed` still holds, because a cancelled request never reached one; cancellations are counted in `window` |
+| other per-step fields, `prefix_cache`, `slo` | The same verdict population as the step counts |
+
+`replay.window` holds the configuration and the counts:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
